 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control was introduced with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023), which established the baseline algorithm for splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D positional gradients [S1, unofficial][S8, unofficial]. Since then, numerous variations have been proposed to address specific limitations such as memory efficiency [S1, unofficial], dynamic scene modeling [S1, unofficial], and compression [S1, unofficial]. The field has evolved from simple gradient-threshold heuristics to more sophisticated methods involving probabilistic sampling [S1, unofficial], Fisher Information-based uncertainty [S1, unofficial], and learnable voxel selection [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of adaptive density control was introduced with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1, unofficial]. The method was designed to handle the trade-off between rendering quality and computational efficiency by dynamically managing the number of Gaussian primitives. Early implementations relied on simple gradient magnitude thresholds and opacity pruning. Subsequent research has expanded this family to include more sophisticated scoring mechanisms (Score-based Densification), probabilistic approaches (3DGS-MCMC), memory-efficient layouts (Locality-preserving Densification), and specialized applications like SLAM (Gaussian Insertion and Pruning) and dynamic scenes (GroupFlow, Adaptive Gaussian Refinement) [S1, S5, S8, unofficial]. The family continues to evolve with methods focusing on compression (Mini-Splatting, GaussianSPA) and automated hyperparameter tuning (TangoGS) [S1, unofficial][S2, S4].
 
